{"id":2024,"date":"2020-12-11T10:13:07","date_gmt":"2020-12-11T18:13:07","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=2024"},"modified":"2024-09-01T13:58:29","modified_gmt":"2024-09-01T20:58:29","slug":"zumi-python-shapes-with-loops","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/zumi-python-shapes-with-loops\/","title":{"rendered":"1.7: Shapes with Loops"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22151a47c75\" name=\"6a22151a47c75\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Shapes with Loops<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_single_image image=&#8221;2027&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div class=\"cell border-box-sizing text_cell rendered\">\n<div class=\"inner_cell\">\n<div class=\"text_cell_render border-box-sizing rendered_html\">\n<p>Usually, certain shapes have a set number of degrees that the internal angles add up to. For example, a square&#8217;s angles add up to 360\u00b0, while a triangle\u2019s angles add up to 180\u00b0. For shapes that have sides of equal length, you will learn how to use a loop to write more efficient code.<\/p>\n<h3>Import libraries<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from zumi.zumi import Zumi\r\nimport time\r\n\r\nzumi = Zumi()<\/pre>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22151a47d77\" name=\"6a22151a47d77\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Square code<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]To appreciate the value of a for loop, first think about the code for driving in a square. Since a square has four equal sides, you would need to go forward then turn left or right four times.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2028&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]Using drive commands from the Basic Drive Commands lesson, write a simple square code.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22151a47e17\" name=\"6a22151a47e17\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Loops<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<p class=\"jupyter-nb-cells-json\"><span style=\"font-size: medium;\">You have probably noticed that you repeat the same section of code four times. That&#8217;s a lot of typing! To get around this, you can use a loop, which lets you repeat parts of your code. They&#8217;re a great shortcut &#8212; without them, you would always have to write the same lines over and over again. They are called loops because the last statement loops around back to the beginning.<br \/>\n<\/span><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2029&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22151a47ea4\" name=\"6a22151a47ea4\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>For loops<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]A for loop will repeat as many times as it is programmed. This means you can choose how many times your code will repeat! Here is the same square code written as a loop in Python. What do you notice?<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">for x in range(4):\r\n    zumi.forward()\r\n    zumi.turn_left()<\/pre>\n<p>In this example, x is a\u00a0<strong>variable<\/strong>, or placeholder, to keep track of how many times the loop has run. What number do you think the loop starts at? Run the cell below and count the outputs. We have separated the outputs with a one-second delay to make it easier to count. See how the loop prints the value of x, our variable, as it increases by one each <strong>iteration<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>In [ ]:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">for number in range(4):\r\n    print(number)\r\n    time.sleep(1)<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: medium;\">Did you see that the variable starts at 0 and ends at 3? In Python, loops start at 0 and increment by 1 by default. At the beginning of the program, x = 0. By the end of the program, x=3. The variable stops at 3 because the loop will continue as long as x is less than the value in the parentheses. Adding one more to three means x=4. 4 is not less than 4, so the loop stops. Try changing the value in the parenthesis to control the number of iterations. What number does it stop at?<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: medium;\">Practice for loops by making a square with four <strong>right<\/strong>\u00a0turns instead of left turns.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22151a47efd\" name=\"6a22151a47efd\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>More shapes!<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]What other shapes can you make with loops? The square is the easiest because each turn is 90 degrees, but what about other shapes? How many sides does a triangle have? This particular triangle will be an\u00a0<strong>equilateral<\/strong>\u00a0triangle, meaning all three sides are of equal length and every angle is the same number of degrees. Here&#8217;s a hint: the exterior angles of all\u00a0<strong>regular<\/strong>\u00a0polygons, or shapes with equal sides, always add up to 360 degrees.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2030&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]There are many other shapes you can teach Zumi. Try pentagons, hexagons, or octagons! You may need a pencil and paper to figure out how many degrees you need to turn. Remember, the number of degrees multiplied by the number of sides should be equal to 360 degrees.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2031&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22151a47f6a\" name=\"6a22151a47f6a\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Extension Activities<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"wp-image-1910 alignleft\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2020\/12\/math_extension-150x150.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"115\" height=\"115\" srcset=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2020\/12\/math_extension-150x150.jpg 150w, https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2020\/12\/math_extension.jpg 240w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 115px) 100vw, 115px\" \/><\/p>\n<h3>Perimeters and areas<\/h3>\n<p>Use tape or a large mat to design a coordinate plane on the floor. Mark when your Zumi stops and turn to determine the points of the shape. Next, measure the length of the shape\u2019s sides and find the perimeter(or circumference) and area of the shape.[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]<img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"alignleft wp-image-1910 \" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2020\/12\/math_extension-150x150.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"115\" height=\"115\" srcset=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2020\/12\/math_extension-150x150.jpg 150w, https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2020\/12\/math_extension.jpg 240w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 115px) 100vw, 115px\" \/><\/p>\n<h3>Transformations<\/h3>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: medium;\">For each shape you program, program a rotation, reflection, or translation of their original shape.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>Rotation<\/strong>\u00a0means a shape is turned around one central point.<br \/>\n<strong>Reflection<\/strong>\u00a0means a shape is flipped over a line.<br \/>\n<strong>Transformation<\/strong>\u00a0means every point of a shape is moved the same distance in the same direction.<\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: medium;\">In all three, the shape still has the same size, area, angles, and line lengths.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 7 Shapes with Loops [vc_single_image image=\"2027\" img_size=\"large\" alignment=\"center\"][vc_column_text] Usually, certain shapes have a set number of degrees that the internal angles add up to. For example, a square's&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/zumi-python-shapes-with-loops\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":2050,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":810,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[],"lesson-tag":[2135,2123,2118,2122,2134,2234,2230],"class_list":["post-2024","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-beginner","lesson-tag-fundamentals","lesson-tag-loops","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","lesson-tag-shapes","lesson-tag-zumi","module-beginner","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/2024","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types